HST PDF: sign encrypted PDFs, keep their encryption, and respect their permissions.

Document systems hand out PDFs encrypted with an owner password only (AES-128 with object
streams, as iText writes them; also RC4 and AES-256). vcl::filter::PDFDocument could not read
their object streams, so the page tree was missing and no signature could be appended.

PDFStandardSecurity is the standard security handler (revisions 2-6). It finds the file key from
an empty, user or owner password and decrypts the object streams and the strings that signing
reads. It also encrypts every string and stream of the objects that the incremental update
appends, with the keys of the file. The signature /Contents is not encrypted, as specified. The
update's trailer keeps /Encrypt and /ID, so the earlier revisions and signatures stay byte for
byte as they were. Nothing is decrypted and rewritten.

Three existing bugs are fixed on the way:
- New object numbers came from the number of xref entries. With gaps in the xref (common in
  xref streams) they reused numbers of existing objects. They now start after the largest
  number and the trailer /Size.
- A page, catalog or AcroForm stored in an object stream was copied from the wrong buffer. It is
  now copied from its own buffer and written as an uncompressed entry.
- Field names in encrypted strings were not seen, so a second "Signature1" could be added.

Permissions follow ISO 32000-2 Table 22, as Acrobat does:
- a new signature field needs bits 4 and 6 (modify; annotations and forms);
- an existing empty signature field can be signed with bit 6 or 9 (fill in forms);
- the owner password allows both.
PDFDocument::GetEmptySignatureFields() and SetSignatureField() sign the empty field. signPdf
uses that field when a new one is not allowed, with an appearance of the field's size.

HST PDF refuses otherwise with "Tệp của người gửi không cho phép ký số", and offers "Nhập mật
khẩu quyền". With the permissions password the file is signed with full access and keeps its
original encryption. A file that needs an open password is signed with the password it was
opened with. Signing from Draw also refuses when new fields are forbidden.

Tests (CppUnit pdfsigning), on samples of tests/runtime/pdf_encrypted_samples.py stored in
base64:
- AES-128 with object streams, AES-256 (R6), RC4 128 and RC4 40: signed twice, both signatures
  verify, the update is encrypted and the file stays encrypted;
- every combination of bits 4, 6 and 9: new field, empty field or refused;
- owner password right and wrong, for each cipher;
- user password.

